prompt:
Scope: front-end implementation for editing (PUT) an entry's description field so that: omitted description in PUT = leave DB value unchanged; description=null in PUT = clear DB NULL; description="" in PUT = store empty string. The front-end must provide an explicit "clear" action that results in sending null, must enforce description max length 255, must not convert the omitted case into null nor collapse empty string into null, and must not change creation (POST) rules.

Explicit requirements and preserved API facts (do not change, repeat here so front-end keeps them):
- PUT behavior that the backend expects:
  - If the JSON body omits the description property entirely, the backend must not modify the row's description.
  - If description is present and null, the backend will set the DB value to NULL.
  - If description is present and a string (including an empty string ""), the backend will set the DB value to that string.
- Front-end must provide an explicit "clear" operation that results in description:null in the PUT body.
- Description string length limit: 255 characters. Enforce client-side validation before sending PUT (do not allow >255 to be submitted for the description string value).
- Do not change creation (POST) rules; do not alter POST behaviour in this task.

Front-end implementation instructions (step-by-step):
1) UI and user actions
   - Keep the existing description input control (single-line or textarea) for editing.
   - Add an explicit UI control to "Clear description" (example: a small button or menu item). Label it clearly as "Clear description (set NULL)" so users know it will store NULL.
   - Do NOT make the standard delete/backspace of the text input implicitly mean "clear to NULL". That should represent an edited value (possibly an empty string) unless the explicit Clear action is used.

2) Maintain initial state and interaction tracking
   - On form load, capture the server-provided initialDescription value exactly as received (could be a string or null or absent). Store it in a variable initialDescription (do not canonicalize null -> "" or vice versa).
   - Keep these client-side state variables at minimum:
     - initialDescription: value from server (null or string or missing)
     - currentDescription: the current text value shown in the input (string). For display you may map null -> "" visually, but keep initialDescription separately so you can detect "untouched".
     - touched (boolean): false initially, set to true on the first user edit event on the description input (input/change/blur after editing). Do NOT set touched when simply focusing the field without changes.
     - explicitClear (boolean): false initially, set to true only when the user activates the explicit "Clear description" control. Clearing must set currentDescription to "" in the UI for clarity, but also set explicitClear = true and touched = true.
   - Important: explicitClear is the only action that must cause the PUT to include description: null. Manual deletion of the input (user deletes all characters) should set currentDescription = "" and touched = true, but explicitClear must be used to send null.

3) Payload construction for PUT (must be exact)
   - Build a new payload object for the PUT. Start with an empty object: const payload = {}.
   - Decide whether to include description in the payload using this exact logic:
     - If explicitClear === true -> payload.description = null.
     - Else if touched === true -> payload.description = currentDescription  (this may be an empty string "").
     - Else -> do NOT set payload.description at all (leave it omitted). Do not set it to undefined either (omit the key entirely).
   - Implementation notes:
     - In JavaScript, do not set payload.description = undefined to omit it; instead simply don't assign the property or delete it before JSON.stringify. JSON.stringify drops properties whose value is undefined; null is serialized as null.
     - Example pattern (pseudo-JS):
       const payload = {};
       if (explicitClear) {
         payload.description = null;
       } else if (touched) {
         payload.description = currentDescription; // may be ""
       } // else leave payload without description property

4) Client-side validation (must happen before sending)
   - If explicitClear === true: do NOT enforce the 255-char length check for description (null has no length).
   - Else if touched === true: enforce that currentDescription.length <= 255. If it exceeds 255, prevent submit and show a validation error message near the input.
   - If touched === false (omitted), no need to validate description (it will not be sent).

5) Submit details and JSON handling
   - Send the constructed payload as application/json in the PUT request body. Use the existing app's networking helper (do not invent an endpoint here). Example: sendPut(payload) or fetch(putUrl, { method: 'PUT', headers: {'Content-Type':'application/json'}, body: JSON.stringify(payload) }).
   - Because JSON.stringify includes null but omits undefined, the payload rules above will produce the correct serialized body: description absent, description: null, or description: "".

6) UI behavior on Clear action
   - When user clicks "Clear description": set explicitClear = true, touched = true, currentDescription = "" (so UI reflects empty field), and update any form validation state. When user cancels or retypes, clear explicitClear if they type new text.
   - If the user clicks Clear then types new text, the code should set explicitClear = false and touched remains true; the PUT should carry the new text.

7) Edge cases and defensive coding
   - If initialDescription is missing from server payload (rare), treat it as "not provided" and keep touched = false until user edits.
   - Do not auto-trim input before deciding whether to send empty string vs omit. Trimming is a separate UX decision; if trimming is applied, clearly document it in the UI. If you do trim, perform trimming only when touched === true and treat trimmed "" as empty string to be sent (not as null), unless explicitClear is used.
   - Do not collapse empty-string values to null anywhere in the client code path (including serialization, API wrapper, or middleware). If you use helper functions that map empty-to-null, disable that behavior for this field.

8) Tests to run (manual or automated)
   - Test A (no-change): Load entry with description = "some text". Do not edit description. Submit PUT. Expected: request JSON has no description property.
   - Test B (explicit clear to NULL): Load entry with description = "some text". Click Clear description control. Submit PUT. Expected: request JSON includes "description": null.
   - Test C (save empty string): Load entry with description = "some text". Delete all characters in the input (but do not use Clear control). touched should become true. Submit PUT. Expected: request JSON includes "description": "".
   - Test D (save new non-empty string): Edit to a new string <=255 chars. Submit. Expected: request JSON includes that string.
   - Test E (length validation): Edit description to a string of length 256. Attempt submit. Expected: client blocks submit and shows validation error; no PUT sent.
   - Test F (initial null but untouched): Load entry with description = null. Do not edit. Submit PUT. Expected: request JSON has no description property (omit key).
   - Test G (initial null then explicit clear): Load with description = null. Click Clear description. Submit. Expected: request JSON includes "description": null (allowed albeit redundant).

9) Logging/troubleshooting
   - Log the payload immediately before JSON.stringify in dev mode to verify the presence/absence/value of description for each case.
   - Add unit/integration tests around the payload-construction function to assert the three distinct outputs (omit / null / string).

10) Do not change create (POST) paths
   - Leave POST creation flows unchanged. If they currently treat empty text or null differently, keep those behaviors intact; this change applies only to the PUT/edit flow described above.

Implementation examples (framework-agnostic pseudocode):
- State initialization:
  initialDescription = serverEntry.description; // may be null or string or missing
  currentDescription = (initialDescription === null ? "" : initialDescription || "");
  touched = false;
  explicitClear = false;
- On input change handler:
  function onDescriptionInputChange(newText) {
    currentDescription = newText;
    if (!touched) touched = true;
    if (explicitClear) explicitClear = false; // user typed after clearing => no longer explicit null
  }
- On Clear button handler:
  function onClearClick() {
    explicitClear = true;
    touched = true;
    currentDescription = ""; // update UI
  }
- On submit handler:
  function onSubmit() {
    // validation
    if (!explicitClear && touched) {
      if (currentDescription.length > 255) { showValidationError('Description must be 255 characters or fewer'); return; }
    }
    const payload = {};
    if (explicitClear) {
      payload.description = null;
    } else if (touched) {
      payload.description = currentDescription; // possibly ""
    } // else omit description

    // send payload via existing PUT helper
    sendPut(payload);
  }

Deliverables for front-end developer:
- Add Clear description control and wire handlers as above.
- Implement touched/explicitClear tracking and payload construction exactly as described.
- Add client-side length validation (<=255) applied only when sending a string value.
- Add tests that assert the PUT body for the cases listed.

Do not invent or assume additional server behavior or API routes; use the application’s existing network helper to perform the PUT with the constructed payload. Ensure logs/tests verify the three distinct serialized outcomes: omitted, null, and string (possibly empty string).
